
  [1mThird question: should [38;5;153mlogin[39m become async now?[0m Today it's synchronous and returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m
  immediately. But you've just chosen "the server returns the userId," and a real network call can't be synchronous.
  This is the single most expensive thing to defer: async-ness is viral, so every call site and every form that waits on
  identity has to be written for it. Doing it later means rewriting all of them.

  - [3mAsync interface now, fake behind it[0m — [38;5;153mlogin[39m returns a Promise and the stub resolves a fixed id after a tick. Every
    call site is written correctly from day one; swapping the fake for a real [38;5;153mfetch[39m touches exactly one function. Costs
    a little ceremony today for code that isn't calling a network yet.
  - [3mStay synchronous, return a fixed id[0m — smallest possible change, reads naturally right now. But it bakes a
    synchronous contract into every form you add, and the day the real API lands you rewrite all of them plus the
    session layer's timing assumptions.
  - [3mImplement the real [38;5;153mfetch[39m now[0m — no fake at all. Honest, but [38;5;153mapi.example.com[39m isn't real, so nothing is runnable or
    testable until there's a backend, and the whole feature is blocked on something outside this repo.

  My recommendation is the async interface with a fake behind it. It's the option where the thing that's hard to change
  (the contract) is right immediately and the thing that's easy to change (what the stub does) stays swappable. It also
  keeps the feature testable today, which the third option doesn't.

